  <h1>Salary Research Guide</h1>
  <p><em>Reviewed by Aspen Lockwood (AL), Editor-in-Chief. Updated May 2026.</em></p>
  <h2>Key salary databases</h2><p>Glassdoor: large dataset, broad coverage, somewhat out of date. Levels.fyi: best for total comp in tech (includes base + bonus + equity by company and level). LinkedIn Salary: good for non-tech roles, filterable by location and experience. Payscale: detailed skills-based data. BLS Occupational Employment Statistics: free government data, updated annually, but less granular than commercial sources.</p>
  <h2>How to read salary ranges</h2><p>Salary ranges at most companies span approximately 50–60% from band minimum to maximum. The midpoint represents the &ldquo;fully proficient&rdquo; level. New hires typically start at the 40th–60th percentile of the band. Negotiating to the 60th–70th percentile is reasonable; asking for above the band maximum is usually pointless.</p>
  <h2>Adjusting for location</h2><p>Raw salary data may not be location-adjusted. Use cost-of-living calculators (NerdWallet, CNN Money) to translate between markets. For remote roles, many companies pay to one location&rsquo;s market rate — ask which market they use.</p>
  <h2>Talking to people in your network</h2><p>The best salary data is from people in similar roles at similar companies. Informational interviews and professional community discussions (Blind, tech communities, professional associations) provide current, specific data that published databases miss. Sharing salary data with peers is legally protected under the NLRA.</p>
